hen JSON-encode it.\n\nWe'll produce the final JSON.\n\nLet me draft the Python code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    payload = case['payload']\n    wti = payload['word_token_indices']\n    wl = payload['word_labels']\n    tc = payload['token_count']\n    # validate lengths\n    if len(wti) != len(wl):\n        return {\"error\": \"mismatch length\"}\n    # initialize\n    token_labels = [None] * tc\n    for i, indices in enumerate(wti):\n        label = wl[i]\n        for idx in indices:\n            if not (0 <= idx < tc):\n                return {\"error\": \"out of range\"}\n            if token_labels[idx] is not None:\n                return {\"error\": \"overlap\"}\n            token_labels[idx] = label\n    # check coverage\n    for idx in range(tc):\n        if token_labels[idx] is None:\n            return {\"error\": \"uncovered token\"}\n    token_labels = [int(x) for x in token_labels]  # ensure ints\n    retained = [i for i, v in enumerate(token_labels) if v == 1]\n    return {\"token_labels\": token_labels, \"retained_indices\": retained}\n\nBut note: token_labels values are already integers (0 or 1). But we can cast.\n\nReturning a dict with those keys.\n\nIf validation fails, we return an error dict. That's acceptable.\n\nWe need to ensure the function does not mutate case. It only reads.\n\nNow, produce JSON with escaped Python source.\n\nWrite the source string with triple quotes? We'll just write as a string literal. For JSON we need to escape newlines. I'll format it as a single line with semicolons separated? Better to keep readable with \\n. We'll produce the code as a multi-line string inside JSON.\n\nExample: \"def solve(case):\\n    payload = case['payload']\\n    ...\" etc.\n\nWe'll write the code with proper indentation using spaces.\n\nI'll write the code exactly as below, then escape for JSON.\n\nLet me write the code block:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    payload = case['payload']\n    wti = payload['word_token_indices']\n    wl = payload['word_labels']\n    tc = payload['token_count']\n    if len(wti) != len(wl):\n        return {\"error\": \"length mismatch\"}\n    token_labels = [None] * tc\n    for i, indices in enumerate(wti):\n        label = wl[i]\n        for idx in indices:\n            if not (0 <= idx < tc):\n                return {\"error\": \"out of range\"}\n            if token_labels[idx] is not None:\n                return {\"error\": \"overlap\"}\n            token_labels[idx] = label\n    for idx in range(tc):\n        if token_labels[idx] is None:\n            return {\"error\": \"uncovered\"}\n    token_labels = [int(x) for x in token_labels]\n    retained = [i for i, v in enumerate(token_labels) if v == 1]\n    return {\"token_labels\": token_labels, \"retained_indices\": retained}\n\nThat's it.\n\nNow we need to JSON-encode this as a string. In Python, we can use json.dumps to escape. But we are writing the final output manually. We'll replace newline with \\n, backslash with \\\\, quotes with \\\". We'll also ensure no trailing spaces.\n\nLet me produce the JSON:\n\n{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    payload = case['payload']\\n    wti = payload['word_token_indices']\\n    wl = payload['word_labels']\\n    tc = payload['token_count']\\n    if len(wti) != len(wl):\\n        return {\\\"error\\\": \\\"length mismatch\\\"}\\n    token_labels = [None] * tc\\n    for i, indices in enumerate(wti):\\n        label = wl[i]\\n        for idx in indices:\\n            if not (0 <= idx < tc):\\n                return {\\\"error\\\": \\\"out of range\\\"}\\n            if token_labels[idx] is not None:\\n                return {\\\"error\\\": \\\"overlap\\\"}\\n            token_labels[idx] = label\\n    for idx in range(tc):\\n        if token_labels[idx] is None:\\n            return {\\\"error\\\": \\\"uncovered\\\"}\\n    token_labels = [int(x) for x in token_labels]\\n    retained = [i for i, v in enumerate(token_labels) if v == 1]\\n    return {\\\"token_labels\\\": token_labels, \\\"retained_indices\\\": retained}\"\n}\n\nNote: Inside the string, we need to escape double quotes. So we have {\\\"error\\\": \\\"length mismatch\\\"} etc. Also newlines are \\n. The key \"implementation\" is the only key.\n\nWe must ensure the first non-whitespace is { and last is }. So we output exactly that JSON without any extra.\n\nLet me double-check: In the Python code, we used double quotes for dictionary keys and string values. In JSON the entire implementation string is enclosed in double quotes, so we need to escape each double quote inside with backslash. We did that. Also we used single quotes for payload['payload']; that's fine, no escape needed. But note: The JSON string will contain single quotes, that's okay. However, the JSON spec allows any character except unescaped control characters and double quotes. Single quotes are fine.\n\nWe also have backslashes? None in code except the ones we added for escaping. We don't have escape sequences in code except the ones we manually placed for JSON. So it's fine.\n\nI'll produce the final JSON as a single object."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":807,"completion_tokens":2941,"total_tokens":3748,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":640},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2679},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":640,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":167},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}